---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CountsTable from "../components/CountsTable.astro";
import ScenarioTable from "../components/ScenarioTable.astro";
import TimingTable from "../components/TimingTable.astro";

// Both tables are rendered FROM the repository's own measurement artefacts,
// not from figures retyped into this file. The counts come from the baseline
// CI gates against on every push, so a number here is a number a machine
// checked. The timing summary is only rendered when a CI run has produced one:
// a microsecond taken on somebody's laptop is not evidence anybody else can
// use, and this page would rather show nothing than show that.
---

<Base
  title="Benchmark — form-contract"
  description="Two lanes: deterministic counts gated in CI, and microseconds from Chrome's own trace that are printed and never gated. The harness publishes its own resolution above its results."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">What the benchmark can, and cannot, see</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Five form runtimes plus a hand-written reference are driven through one
    scripted transcript against{" "}
    <strong class="text-on-surface">one zod schema the harness owns and
    instruments</strong>, rendering{" "}
    <strong class="text-on-surface">one DOM the harness owns and hashes</strong>.
    No count is produced by code an adapter author wrote: renders come from
    fiber alternates, validation work from a harness-owned schema wrapper, DOM
    changes from a <code>MutationObserver</code>. There is no counter to place
    favourably.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Nothing is printed until six proofs hold — that every subject reached the
    same schema instance, judged the same root, rendered the same DOM, is
    actually wired up, and that the oracle would have noticed had it not been.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== counts -->
  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The counts lane, which is gated</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Commits, changed fibers, DOM mutations and validator passes are integers
    that do not depend on the machine, which is the only reason a CI gate can
    exist at all. A drift is therefore never noise, and CI fails on one.
  </p>

  <CountsTable />

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>validatorPasses</code> and <code>paths judged</code> sit on every row
    beside the render counts, so a zero-commit row can never be read as free.
    The two react-hook-form rows above commit nothing and re-render nothing for
    a keystroke — <code>register</code> leaves the inputs uncontrolled — while
    still handing the whole 201-path root to the schema to find out whether the
    verdict moved. The <code>on-submit</code> row does not even do that, by
    documented design, which is what its agreement cell records.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Read the fiber column carefully: it is{" "}
    <strong class="text-on-surface">leaves + a constant</strong>, and the
    constant is what differs. At 31, 61 and 201 fields the hand-written
    reference reports 39, 69 and 209 — always the field count plus eight. That
    is React cloning the whole sibling list of the form it had to descend
    through, and every implementation that commits at all pays it. What
    form-contract adds on top is 3, at every size.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    So the question worth asking is not how much a commit costs but whether one
    happens. <code>useField</code> subscribes to the value cell, which makes the
    input controlled and a keystroke a render.{" "}
    <code>useUncontrolledField</code> subscribes to the same cell imperatively
    and writes the DOM node instead, which is what
    <code>register</code> does for react-hook-form and why both read zero above.
  </p>

  <ScenarioTable />

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The middle column is where the deferred pass shows up as a cost rather than
    a saving. With <code>useField</code> the value lands in one task and the
    verdict in the next, so React cannot batch them and commits twice.
    Uncontrolled, the value write stops being React's business and only the
    verdict commits.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The last column is the one the whole-root pass was built for. A rule
    reporting against a field nobody touched costs form-contract one commit and
    one schema pass. react-hook-form in its scoped configuration does not show
    it at all; the <code>deps</code> configuration that does costs 1213 changed
    fibers, two passes and 402 paths judged.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== time -->
  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The timing lane, which is never gated</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Headless Chrome over CDP, every figure taken from the browser's own trace
    rather than from page JavaScript. 21 interleaved pairs per comparison, order
    alternating every pair, both members of a pair inside one tracing session on
    two origins so that they are two renderer processes.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Before it compares anything it measures itself: a known cost injected at
    seven sizes and three scheduling positions, and every subject run against a
    byte-identical twin of itself at every size. A band that overlaps the twin's
    band prints <code>indistinguishable</code>, never a win.
  </p>

  <TimingTable />

  <!-- ================================================== excluded -->
  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">What was measured and then thrown away</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Each excluded metric is published with the measurement that excluded it, so
    a reader can check whether the discarded ones happen to be the flattering
    ones. <code>ScriptDuration</code> showed a 245% spread between two
    byte-identical pages. <code>TaskDuration</code> is dominated by the driver's
    own CDP round trip. <code>PerformanceEventTiming.duration</code> is quantised
    to 8 ms, larger than everything the lane publishes.
    React's <code>Profiler.actualDuration</code> excludes commit and fires zero
    times in the production build this lane requires. And every millisecond
    taken in jsdom, because jsdom has no layout, no style resolution and no
    paint — on a page like this one, layout alone is routinely larger than the
    script that precedes it.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Both lanes are run by <code>npm run bench:forms</code> and{" "}
    <code>npm run bench:forms:time</code>, and the design that argues for every
    choice above is in <code>docs/design/form-benchmark.md</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
